        </div>
        <p>We can construct two vectors {`\\(x\\hat{i}\\)`} and {`\\(y\\hat{j}\\)`} using the co-ordinates of P and the unit vectors along the two axes. Using the parallelogram law of vector addition, we can write {`\\[\\vec{p} = x\\hat{i} + y\\hat{j}\\]`} This shows that any point in an XY-plane can be represented by a vector. The two parts {`\\(x\\hat{i}\\)`} and {`\\(y\\hat{j}\\)`} are called <em>components</em> of {`\\(\\vec{p}\\)`} along {`\\(x\\)`}- and {`\\(y\\)`}-axes, respectively.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
        <h2>Resolving a vector</h2>
        <p>Using the process above, we can express any vector {`\\(\\vec{a}\\)`} as sum of two component vectors {`\\(a_x\\hat{i}\\)`} and {`\\(a_y\\hat{j}\\)`} and write {`\\[\\vec{a} = a_x\\hat{i} + a_y\\hat{j}\\]`}</p>
