        </div>
        <p>Assuming that the vector {`\\(\\vec{a}\\)`} makes an angle of {`\\(\\theta\\)`} with the {`\\(x\\)`}-axis, we can show that {`\\[
            \\begin{eqnarray}
            a_x &=& a\\cos\\theta\\\\
            a_y &=& a\\sin\\theta
            \\end{eqnarray}
            \\]`}
        </p>
        <p>This process of writing a vector as sum of components along co-ordinate axes is called <em>resolving</em> the vector. Later on, we'll see resolving a vector along co-ordinate axes simplifies vector algebra a lot.</p>
        <p>
            {`\\(a_x\\)`} is called <em>projection</em> of {`\\(\\vec{a}\\)`} along {`\\(x\\)`}-axis and {`\\(a_y\\)`} is called <em>projection</em> of {`\\(\\vec{a}\\)`} along {`\\(y\\)`}-axis.
        </p>
    </section>
    <section>
        <h2>Vectors in three dimensions</h2>
        <p>
            In three dimensions, we need three {`\\(x\\)`}-, {`\\(y\\)`}- and {`\\(z\\)`}-axes to specify a point P as {`\\((x, y, z)\\)`}. Just like two dimensions above, any point in three dimensions can be represented as a vector. The main difference is in the resolution of vectors. Now, they have three components, one along each axis. {`\\[\\vec{a} = a_x\\hat{i} + a_y\\hat{j} + a_z\\hat{k}\\]`} where {`\\(\\hat{k}\\)`} is the unit vector along the positive {`\\(z\\)`}-axis.
        </p>
