        </div>
        <p>
            Given vector {`\\(\\vec{a}\\)`} in three dimensions such that the tail of {`\\(\\vec{a}\\)`} lies at origin O and the head lies at a point P, suppose the angle between {`\\(\\vec{a}\\)`} and the {`\\(z\\)`}-axis be {`\\(\\theta\\)`}. Consider the projecton of P onto the XY-plane shown by point Q. (It is obtained by drawing a line parallel to {`\\(z\\)`}-axis passing through P and hitting the XY-plane at Q.) Suppose the angle between OQ and the {`\\(x\\)`}-axis by {`\\(\\phi\\)`}. By pythagorus theorem, {`\\[OQ = a\\sin\\theta\\]`}
            Another application of the theorem gives {`
                \\begin{eqnarray}
                    a_x &=& a\\sin\\theta\\cos\\phi\\\\
                    a_y &=& a\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi\\\\
                    a_z &=& a\\cos\\theta
                \\end{eqnarray}
            `}
        </p>
